<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<ValidationRule xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        THIS NOTE LIVES IN A COMMENT, NOT IN <description>, AND IT MUST STAY THAT WAY.
        ValidationRule.description is capped at 255 characters. The full rationale below is
        operationally important, so it lives here rather than being trimmed; a comment is
        repo-only and is never sent to the Metadata API. It is INSIDE the root element on
        purpose - a comment ABOVE <ValidationRule> breaks `sf` deploy at conversion with the
        misleading error "unable to find matching parent xml file". Same shape as the house
        precedent, Contract_Signed_Before_Closed_Won.

        =====================================================================
        🔴 THE FSD'S STATED RATIONALE IS WRONG. THIS COMMENT CORRECTS IT RATHER THAN
        PROPAGATING IT INTO METADATA (Gate 1, Q3.4 = accept).
        =====================================================================
        FSD Gap Tranche 1 §29.1 asks for this rule so the Day-0 75-task fan-out cannot fire
        early. It does NOT: flows/Transaction_Task_Fanout.flow-meta.xml is RecordAfterSave on
        Transaction__c, filtered on Transaction__c.Contract_Executed_Date__c IsNull=false AND
        Tasks_Fanned_Out__c EqualTo false. It never reads Opportunity.Contract_Executed_Date__c
        at all, so a rule on THIS field cannot arm or disarm that flow.

        WHAT THIS RULE ACTUALLY PREVENTS: a MIS-DATED 75-task checklist, not an early one.
        ContractExecutionService.openTransactionsOnAboutToClose copies
        Opportunity.Contract_Executed_Date__c into Transaction__c.Contract_Executed_Date__c as
        Day 0, and every one of the ~75 checklist tasks is dated by offset from that value. A
        hand-typed Opportunity date - entered before the PSA is actually executed - produces a
        checklist whose every deadline is silently wrong, not a checklist that never should have
        existed. That is a real defect worth guarding against; it is simply not the defect the
        FSD named.

        A companion rule directly on Transaction__c.Contract_Executed_Date__c, which WOULD gate
        the fan-out itself, is a deliberate tranche-2 candidate (Gate 1, Q3.4 recommendation
        (a), option (b) declined for this tranche) - see the flagged-findings list in
        agent-output/design-requirements-fsd-tranche-1.md. It needs its own design pass: that
        field is set at INSERT by openTransactionsOnAboutToClose with allOrNone=true inside an
        Opportunity after-update trigger, so a validation-rule block there would roll back the
        deal's own stage change, and the rule would need a two-hop
        Opportunity__r.Primary_Contract__r.Negotiation_Status__c reference from Transaction__c.

        =====================================================================
        WHY Negotiation_Status__c AND NOT Stage__c.
        =====================================================================
        Contract_Review__c.Stage__c is RECOMPUTED by the before-save flow
        Contract_Review_Stage_Sync on every save. A direct write to Stage__c commits and is
        silently discarded (measured repo trap, recorded on Contract_Review__c CTR-0000).
        Negotiation_Status__c is the field the deal driver actually moves via the Advance Stage
        action (RecordStageAdvanceService), and 'Executed' is its terminal value on BOTH the
        Acquisition_PSA and Disposition_PSA record types (see the XML comment on
        Contract_Review__c.Negotiation_Status__c).

        =====================================================================
        🔴 NOT(ISBLANK(Primary_Contract__c)) IS MANDATORY, NOT DEFENSIVE POLISH.
        =====================================================================
        ContractExecutionService.stampOpportunities writes Contract_Executed_Date__c via
        Database.update(oppUpdates, true, AccessLevel.SYSTEM_MODE) - ALL-OR-NONE - from inside a
        Contract_Review__c AFTER-UPDATE trigger. AccessLevel.SYSTEM_MODE bypasses CRUD and FLS;
        it does NOT bypass validation rules. Opportunity.Primary_Contract__c is stamped by
        OpportunityReviewService only for the Contract Review IT creates on entry to
        'Under Contract (PSA)'. A Contract Review created any other way - by hand, by a data
        load, on a deal that reached PSA by an unusual route - executes without ever having been
        stamped as the primary. Without this clause, Primary_Contract__r.Negotiation_Status__c
        would be blank, the rule would fire, and the all-or-none update would throw and roll
        back the ENTIRE PSA execution: Contract_Signed__c, the Day-0 stamp, and the three
        GroupNotifier notifications (Investor Relations, Transactions, Due Diligence) - all of
        it, on a deal that just legitimately executed its PSA.

        STATED COST, NOT HIDDEN: with this clause, the rule is UNENFORCEABLE on exactly the
        deals with no primary contract - it fails OPEN on the irregular path and protects only
        the ordinary one. That is the correct trade (a rolled-back PSA execution is worse than
        an unguarded manual date on an irregular deal), but it is a decision, not an oversight.

        A second, independent reason the exemption must ship: ContractExecutionService.
        stampOpportunities has NO catch of its own, so a DmlException from a blocked update
        propagates out of the trigger. A user executing the PSA through the Advance Stage action
        hits RecordStageAdvanceController, which converts EVERY exception into one fixed generic
        string ("This change could not be saved") - so this rule's errorMessage would never
        reach the user who tripped it through that path; only a direct edit or Data Loader run
        would ever see the real text.

        =====================================================================
        ORDERING - MUST BE PROVEN, NOT ASSUMED (post-deploy gate G5).
        =====================================================================
        stampOpportunities runs from ContractReviewTrigger AFTER UPDATE, i.e. after the
        Contract Review row is written to the database within the same transaction, so the
        cross-object formula reference Primary_Contract__r.Negotiation_Status__c should evaluate
        against the already-committed 'Executed' value. If it does not, the all-or-none
        Opportunity update throws inside the after-update trigger and rolls back the whole PSA
        execution. Run ContractExecutionServiceTest end-to-end IMMEDIATELY after this rule
        deploys - a green run there IS the proof this ordering works; do not ship without it.

        =====================================================================
        WRITERS THIS RULE MEETS.
        =====================================================================
        - ContractExecutionService.stampOpportunities (Apex, all-or-none, SYSTEM_MODE): FIRES,
          and a block here is FATAL to the whole PSA execution absent the Primary_Contract__c
          exemption above. The primary hazard this rule was built against.
        - Manual edit / inline edit / Data Loader: FIRES - the population this rule exists for.
        - flows/Transaction_Complete_Close: does NOT write this field. Verified 2026-08-16:
          flows/Transaction_Task_Fanout.flow-meta.xml is the ONLY flow in the repo referencing
          Contract_Executed_Date__c (on Transaction__c, a different object entirely).
        - Approval-process field updates: do not fire validation rules in this org (measured,
          see ApprovalAuditService's class header and ARCHITECTURE.md §2).
        - INSERT: does NOT fire - ISCHANGED() is false on record creation. Deliberate hole, the
          same shape the deployed NDA-adjacent rules in this repo accept.

        =====================================================================
        BYPASS: DELIBERATELY NONE (Gate 1, Q3.2 = accept "no bypass").
        =====================================================================
        The exemption this rule needs is STRUCTURAL (a blank Primary_Contract__c), not
        persona-based, so there is no population a bypass would legitimately serve.

        =====================================================================
        🔴 CODE REVIEW FIX 3 (W1, 2026-08-16) - A SECOND, UNCLOSED STRUCTURAL HOLE, WITH THE
        IDENTICAL FATAL CONSEQUENCE. THE EXEMPTION ANALYSIS ABOVE IS NOT COMPLETE.
        =====================================================================
        The blank-Primary_Contract__c exemption above answers "what if there is NO primary
        contract?". It does not answer a second question with the same shape: what if there
        are TWO Contract Reviews on the same deal and the one that executes is NOT the primary?

        ContractExecutionService.handleExecution collects Contract_Review__c.Opportunity__c for
        ANY Contract Review that just transitioned to Executed - not only the primary one - but
        this rule tests Primary_Contract__r.Negotiation_Status__c specifically. Those need not
        be the same row: Contract_Review__c -> Opportunity is a plain Lookup, so an Opportunity
        can carry more than one Contract Review, and OpportunityReviewService stamps
        Primary_Contract__c only for the child it creates on entry to 'Under Contract (PSA)'.
        A hand-created second Contract Review (CR#2) on a deal that already has its primary
        (CR#1, not executed) is unremarkable and unblocked by anything else in this schema.

        CONCRETE SEQUENCE: deal has CR#1 (primary, still Negotiating) and CR#2 (hand-created,
        not primary). A user executes CR#2. ContractReviewTrigger fires, handleExecution
        collects this Opportunity into executedOppIds, and stampOpportunities writes
        Contract_Executed_Date__c via Database.update(oppUpdates, true, SYSTEM_MODE). This rule
        then evaluates Primary_Contract__r.Negotiation_Status__c - which is CR#1's status, still
        NOT 'Executed' - so the rule FIRES, the all-or-none update throws, and THE ENTIRE PSA
        EXECUTION ROLLS BACK: Contract_Signed__c, the Day-0 stamp, and the three GroupNotifier
        notifications, all of it, on a deal whose PSA (CR#2) just legitimately executed. This is
        the exact outcome this rule exists to prevent, produced by a path the blank-primary
        exemption does not cover: ISBLANK(Primary_Contract__c) is FALSE here (CR#1 is stamped as
        primary), so that clause does not save this case. As with the blank-primary case,
        stampOpportunities has no catch of its own, so a user hitting this through the Advance
        Stage action sees only RecordStageAdvanceController's fixed generic "This change could
        not be saved" - no indication that the wrong Contract Review was the one they executed.

        THIS IS ACCEPTED AS A KNOWN LIMITATION, NOT MISSED. Found in code review 2026-08-16
        (Fix 3 / W1) against the version of this rule already deployed to this branch. Per the
        explicit decision already taken at that review: DO NOT narrow this rule to close it here.
        A validation rule cannot count or aggregate child records, so there is no formula-only
        way to ask "is ANY Contract Review on this Opportunity Executed?" - only "is THE PRIMARY
        one Executed?" - and no rollup of executed-PSA-count exists on Opportunity today. Closing
        this properly needs a new rollup field (e.g. a Number or Formula on Opportunity counting
        or flagging Contract Review children with Negotiation_Status__c = 'Executed', maintained
        by a trigger or declarative rollup) and this rule re-targeted to test THAT field instead
        of Primary_Contract__r.Negotiation_Status__c - which is its own design pass and is out of
        scope for this tranche.

        STATE THIS PLAINLY: the analysis above (writers this rule meets, the blank-primary
        exemption, the ordering proof) covers the ORDINARY one-Contract-Review-per-deal path and
        the ZERO-primary irregular path. It does NOT cover the TWO-OR-MORE-Contract-Reviews
        irregular path, and a reader should not treat the exemption analysis in this file as
        exhaustive just because it is thorough. This paragraph exists so that gap is found here,
        in review, rather than in a rolled-back production PSA execution.
    -->
    <fullName>Executed_PSA_Before_Contract_Date</fullName>
    <active>true</active>
    <description>Blocks setting/changing Contract_Executed_Date__c unless the deal's Primary Contract Review is Executed. Guards against a mis-dated 75-task Transaction checklist, not the fan-out itself. See the XML comment in this file for full rationale.</description>
    <errorConditionFormula>AND(
    ISCHANGED(Contract_Executed_Date__c),
    NOT(ISBLANK(Contract_Executed_Date__c)),
    NOT(ISBLANK(Primary_Contract__c)),
    TEXT(Primary_Contract__r.Negotiation_Status__c) &lt;&gt; &apos;Executed&apos;
)</errorConditionFormula>
    <errorDisplayField>Contract_Executed_Date__c</errorDisplayField>
    <errorMessage>Contract Executed Date can only be set once the PSA is Executed. Execute the PSA on the Contract Review first.</errorMessage>
</ValidationRule>
